<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?>
<assembly xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v1" manifestVersion="1.0">
  <!--
    asInvoker: run with whatever privilege level launched the process,
    same as any normal (non-installer) application - no UAC elevation
    prompt on launch.

    Without ANY manifest at all, Windows applies "installer detection
    heuristics" to unmanifested executables - if it looks like an
    installer (by name, or by writing to a protected directory like
    Program Files), Windows silently virtualizes file/registry writes
    and/or shows the UAC shield and prompts for elevation on every
    launch, not just the first. Any explicit manifest, even this
    minimal one, disables that heuristic entirely (Edward, 2026 -
    matches the same asInvoker fix used for WoWRevived's UAC
    virtualization issue).

    This intentionally does NOT request elevation for the first-run
    install step either - if the user picks an install location that
    genuinely needs elevated permissions to write to, that's Windows'
    own file-system permission system to surface, not something this
    manifest tries to special-case.
  -->
  <trustInfo xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
    <security>
      <requestedPrivileges>
        <requestedExecutionLevel level="asInvoker" uiAccess="false" />
      </requestedPrivileges>
    </security>
  </trustInfo>
</assembly>
